Explain the difference between PUT and PATCH. One AeroPay situation where each is the right method. Answer: PUT replaces the entire resource with the new data, so you send the full updated object every time. PATCH changes only specific fields you specify, leaving the rest unchanged. At AeroPay, use PUT when updating a complete payment profile, and PATCH when just updating a user’s contact info or status without touching other details.
Why does idempotency matter when syncing a config file into a registry, and how did your sync_registry.py ensure it? Answer: Idempotency means running the same sync multiple times gives the same result as running it once, preventing duplicate entries or overwrites. It matters because network retries or restarts could otherwise create duplicates or corrupt configs.  sync_registry.py  ensures this by checking if a service already exists before creating it, and only updating when values actually change.
 For storing service config, give one strength and one weakness of each: JSON, YAML, XML. Answer: Strengths & Weaknesses JSON is lightweight and works easily with nearly all programming languages and web APIs, but it lacks support for comments and can be hard for humans to read quickly. YAML is very clean and readable, supports comments, and uses simple indentation, yet it is sensitive to spacing errors and parses more slowly. XML enforces strict structure with schemas and handles deeply nested data well, but it is wordy, larger in file size, and less commonly used in modern lightweight APIs.
 The registry replies 401, then 404, then 500. What does each mean, and what would you check first for each? Answer:A 401 status means the request lacks valid authentication, so the first thing to check is whether your access token or credentials are present, correct, and not expired. A 404 means the requested service or endpoint cannot be found, so you should verify the URL path and confirm that the specific service or resource actually exists in the registry. A 500 error indicates an unexpected failure on the server side, so the first step is to check server logs or status because this is usually not caused by something wrong in your request.
  Why is a local mock API (like this one) useful during development, and one thing a mock cannot verify? Answer:A local mock API is extremely useful because it lets you build and test your code without needing a live production server, works offline, and lets you simulate different responses quickly and safely. It speeds up development and avoids affecting real systems while you are still building features. However, a mock cannot verify real network delays, actual authentication behavior, performance under heavy load, or how your code truly behaves when integrated with the live service.